ector of spatial  frequencies and this is a vector of  Fourier coefficients and I have to  multiply them element wise to get a new  vector essentially you know kappa 1 f 1  hat kappa 2 f 2 hat and so on and so  forth all of this times i so that's how  i'm defining this this derivative using  the discrete Fourier transform I compute  a vector of frequencies Kappa I have my  vector of Fourier transform coefficients  F hat and I kind of dot multiply them  element wise to get this this new vector  of my derivatives and if I inverse  Fourier transform this quantity when I  inverse Fourier transform I'll recover  my derivative DF DX and what we're gonna  find is that this is actually very very  accurate okay so that's all we're doing  here is I'm creating a big vector of  frequencies in Kappa and this is kind of  a MATLAB trick a couple of lines you  need to know in MATLAB is that if you  define your fundamental frequency as 2  pi divided by L and you can have kind of  low frequencies and high frequencies as  a whole vector of frequencies you have  to do this fft shift command in matlab  which basically reorders this kappa  vector to be consistent with its  internal FFT ordering of the frequencies  I think this is kind of a mess every  language orders frequencies differently  like C++ orders in one way Python you  know and MATLAB will order them  differently and so in MATLAB this is  just AI remember this these two lines of  code you can just copy and paste this  this creates your vector of frequencies  and this shifts then this reorders them  to be consistent with matlab's FFT that  creates this Kappa vector of frequencies  so that each frequency each element of F  has its corresponding frequency that it  corresponds to and now the derivative is  really easy you just take I times Kappa  times F hat and notice this is a dot  times  which does this element-wise  multiplication and then finally all I  have to do is inverse Fourier transform  that and I recover my derivative