 the data consider  the FFT derivative stays quite accurate  okay so that's really nice  property of the FFT okay good now this  wasn't meant to be really quantitative  or exhaustive sure you could come up  with better finite difference schemes  you could make Delta X smaller you could  do all kinds of things we do talk about  this in the book so you can kind of  check this out  let me find it real quick here it is so  we do talk about this in the book where  what I do is I actually carefully go  through and compute the finite  difference derivative and the spectral  or FFT derivative as I increase the  number of points and which corresponds  to decrease in Delta X so we know that  our finite difference is going to get  better when I make my Delta X smaller  and smaller and smaller but how much  better does it get how fast okay so  that's what this plot shows is the error  in a log plot versus how many data  points I have okay and so you're  absolutely right as I increase the  number of data points my my finite  difference does get more accurate  because Delta X is getting smaller but  it gets more accurate very very slowly  whereas the spectral derivative when I  increase my data points it gets much  much much better much much faster okay  so this is is kind of spectral  convergence that's what we call this as  spectral convergence it's much better  than a finite difference scheme in fact  it's better than any finite difference  scheme you know even if I did central  difference or higher order that would  bring these down but the spectral  derivative is kind of you know  outperforming all of these so this is a  nice in fact I would encourage you to  try to write this code yourself it's  really not that hard you just wrap a for  loop around this whole thing making and  larger and larger and larger and then  you can make these plots yourself you  can try different derivative schemes and  really convince yourself this spectral  derivative is is you know much more  accurate at least on clean data okay the  other thing I want to point out and this  is in also in Chapter two of our book is  you do have to be careful when you're  competing the derivative of functions  even if the your function f is  continuous like this function our  triangular hat function we look  earlier the derivative of this  triangular hat function is discontinuous  I have 0 then the derivative immediately  becomes 1 then it immediately becomes  negative 1 and then it goes back to 0 so  there's these jump discontinuities and  when I use the spectral derivative the  FFT to compute the derivative of this  function because the derivative is  discontinuous I'm going to get these  Gibbs phenomena that I that I saw before  ok so you still can get Gibbs phenomenon  if you're taking the derivative of a  function where the derivative is  discontinuous ok so you have to be  careful and if you see this now you'll  know what you're looking at you're  looking at these Gibbs phenomena because  your derivative function is  discontinuous ok so long story short we  can use the FFT to very very accurately  and rapidly approximate derivatives of  vectors of data and we're going to be  able to use this to solve partial  differential equations ok thank you

